Subject: [PATCH] ctrl: RemoveDevice retires the device's ems and keeps its model until nothing holds it

A RemoveDevice (em_cmd_type_remove_device: db_delete, em_delete, dm_delete) freed memory other
threads still used. The em step deleted only dm->get_em(), the em that last set itself on the
command's model copy, and deleted it at once (delete_node), so an agent's other radios kept ems
pointing at its model. The model step then freed the model (delete_data_model) while the network
topology still held it and those ems still ran on it (the bus's method handlers and the ems take
no lock on the list): a use-after-free on the next topology publish or the next frame.

The em step now retires every em whose model is the device's (retire_ems_of_model, as a silent
agent's ems are retired: out of the map and of the commands, stopped, kept allocated). The model
step takes the model out of the topology and the list under the topology lock (unlink_device),
keeps it with the retired agents' models, freed by free_retired_models once it is old enough and
no em nor command holds it, and republishes the topology as the retire paths do. A RemoveDevice
for a device gone meanwhile changes nothing.

The node map's last reads outside its lock, all on the main thread, now take it: the em_config
candidate lookup (build_candidates, find_node), the AP metrics request's radio walk
(handle_ap_metrics_req, get_nodes) and the dev-test encode and decode walks (under m_mutex around
the call; neither takes another lock nor waits). 0280 left them on the ground that only the main
thread removes nodes, but the nodes listener inserts them (create_node), and the map keeps one
cursor for every walker, so another thread's locked walk moved an unlocked one on the main thread:
a skipped or repeated node. em_mgr.h's note says so now.

Test: remove_device_retires_every_em_and_keeps_the_model (a two-radio agent: both ems retired, the
model out of the list and the topology, kept and still readable under ASan, one publish; a second
RemoveDevice changes nothing).
